In an RESP, these usually mean:

EAP = Educational Assistance Payment
This is the growth and grant portion paid out for school. It can include RESP investment earnings, CESG, CLB, and certain provincial incentives. It is generally taxable to the student/beneficiary, not the subscriber.  ￼

PSE = Post-Secondary Education withdrawal/payment
This is a withdrawal of the original contributions once the beneficiary is enrolled in eligible post-secondary studies. Since RESP contributions were made with after-tax money, a PSE withdrawal is generally not taxable when withdrawn. Government material refers to these as Post-Secondary Education withdrawals.  ￼

AIP = Accumulated Income Payment
This is when the investment income/growth in the RESP is paid out, usually to the subscriber, when the money is not being used by the beneficiary for education. An AIP is taxed as the subscriber’s income, and there is also an extra 20% tax federally, or 12% for Quebec residents.  ￼

NCW = Non-Qualified Withdrawal
This is not a main CRA-defined public category the way EAP and AIP are, but in practice firms often use it to mean a withdrawal that is not a qualifying education withdrawal—usually a contribution withdrawal taken when no beneficiary is eligible for an EAP. Those contribution withdrawals themselves are generally not taxable, but they can trigger repayment of grants and related consequences.  ￼

A simple way to think about it:
	•	PSE = take out your own contributions
	•	EAP = take out the grants + growth for the student
	•	AIP = take out the growth when education is not happening
	•	NCW = a non-education / non-qualifying withdrawal label some firms use

